## 2. Distributed Storage: Taming the Disks (HDFS)

Once you decide to store data across 1,000 separate computers, you face immediate structural challenges:
* How do you store a file that is bigger than any single computer’s hard drive?
* Cheap computers crash frequently. What happens when a hard drive burns out mid-calculation?

This is why **HDFS (Hadoop Distributed File System)** was engineered.

```
                           +--------------------------+
                           |  NameNode (The Master)   |
                           |  - Directory Tree (RAM)  |
                           |  - Block Mapping (RAM)   |
                           |  - EditLog / FsImage     |
                           +------------+-------------+
                                        | Heartbeats & Block Reports
         +------------------------------+------------------------------+
         |                              |                              |
         v                              v                              v
+-----------------+            +-----------------+            +-----------------+
| DataNode 1      |            | DataNode 2      |            | DataNode 3      |
| [Block A (128M)]|            | [Block A (Rep)] |            | [Block B (128M)]|
| [Block B (Rep)] |            | [Block C (128M)]|            | [Block A (Rep)] |
+-----------------+            +-----------------+            +-----------------+
```

### The Block Architecture
Your operating system normally divides files into tiny 4 KB chunks. HDFS divides files into massive chunks—typically **128 MB or 256 MB**.

*Why so massive?* 
A mechanical disk has a physical read head that must physically move to locate the beginning of a file (**disk seek**), which takes about 10 ms. If your blocks are tiny (4 KB), the disk spends as much time moving its head around as it does transferring data. By making blocks 128 MB, the disk spends over 99% of its operational time streaming continuous data at maximum speed. Furthermore, larger blocks mean fewer total blocks for the central coordinator to track.

### The Master-Worker Dynamic
HDFS uses a clear division of labor:
* **The Master (NameNode):** 
  * It does **not** store any actual user data. 
  * It is the librarian. It holds the entire filesystem metadata inside its **RAM**: directory structure, permissions, and a lookup table of which 128 MB block numbers reside on which physical machines.
  * Because everything is in RAM, lookup queries are sub-millisecond fast.
* **The Workers (DataNodes):** 
  * The actual workhorses. They store the raw 128 MB blocks on their local disks as plain files and serve read/write requests from clients.
  * Every few seconds, each DataNode sends a **Heartbeat** message to the NameNode: *"I am alive, and here is the list of blocks I am currently holding."*

### Resilience and the Fault-Tolerance Model
Because hardware in large clusters fails continuously, HDFS enforces a **Replication Factor** (default is **3**).
* When a 128 MB block enters the cluster, HDFS writes it to DataNode 1, copies it to DataNode 2 (ideally on the same rack), and copies it to DataNode 3 (on an entirely different rack, guarding against a complete rack switch or power failure).
* If DataNode 1 dies, the NameNode detects the missing heartbeat, looks up its metadata, notices that Block A now only has 2 copies instead of 3, and commands DataNode 2 to replicate Block A to DataNode 4. Self-healing occurs automatically with zero data loss.

### The Secondary NameNode (The Common Misconception)
A frequent misunderstanding is assuming the Secondary NameNode is a hot standby/backup. **It is not.**
* The NameNode logs every file creation or deletion in an immediate transaction log called the `EditLog`. 
* Periodically, these changes must be frozen and merged into the main filesystem image file (`FsImage`).
* If the primary NameNode did this heavy merging itself, it would freeze the cluster. The **Secondary NameNode** acts as an administrative assistant: it downloads the `FsImage` and `EditLog`, merges them cleanly in its own memory, and uploads the compact, fresh checkpoint back to the NameNode.

